# Infrastructure agent

Generated with Dome {{.Version}} for project module `{{.Module}}`.
This names your project; it does not need to be hosted online.
Requires Linux with Go 1.26.11 or newer, locally or in Docker. The optional
`.devcontainer/` supplies pinned Go; use Dev Container CLI or VS Code with the
Dev Containers extension to open it. Codex is optional for conversational authoring.

Optionally, from the host project directory, start the generated workspace:

```sh
devcontainer up --workspace-folder .
devcontainer exec --workspace-folder . bash
```

Alternatively, open this folder in VS Code and run **Dev Containers: Reopen in
Container**, then open its terminal. Run the remaining commands from the project directory
with local Go, or at `/workspace` inside the container. `sandbox/` must remain present.

```sh
CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o agent ./cmd/agent
go test ./...
./agent init --config config.yaml --non-interactive
./agent plan --json
# When you want to apply the inspected changes:
./agent apply --json
./agent status
```

To re-enter later, run the same CLI commands or reopen the folder in VS Code.
The workspace uses a named non-root `dev` user with local UID/GID alignment. Its
bind mount keeps project files and state on your host; keep using `/workspace`
inside the container to preserve resource identities. Creation/startup never
applies resources and the container has no host Docker socket.

To stop a CLI-started workspace, get its container ID from `devcontainer up`'s
JSON output and run `docker stop <container-id>` on the host. For VS Code, close
the remote connection; the configuration stops that container.

If your environment requires a proxy, standard HTTP(S) proxy environment variables
are forwarded by reference. A proxy hostname only known to the host may need an
explicit Docker host mapping in `runArgs`, such as `--add-host=proxy:HOST_IP`.
Configure its real reachable address; do not change download sources to bypass
network failures.

The starter manages only `sandbox/hello.txt`, with mode `0640`, and stores state
under `.dome/`. Edit `config.yaml` to change content. Set `managed: false` to
prune the previously owned file. Running plan never applies host resource changes,
but can create the SQLite state database. Do not delete state to fix failures.

Open Codex in this directory and ask it to use the `dome` skill. Ask for resources
or playbooks in Go, then review the JSON plan before requesting apply.
Adding the Go dependency alone does not install this skill. Restart the coding
agent if it has not discovered the new project-local skill.

Use the installed API with `go doc github.com/jani123/dome/agent` from this
module, so documentation matches its selected dependency. Versioned guides will
be published at https://jani123.github.io/dome/{{.GuideVersion}}/ .
Development guides can move ahead of a pinned development snapshot; use local
`go doc` for its exact API.

## Updates

Keep a clean commit/backup. Run the initializer at the target exact release or resolved Go pseudo-version into a temporary sibling directory, review its skill, instructions,
manifest, and starter against your project, then update the dependency and matching
assets together. Preserve your authored resources and playbooks. Run `go mod tidy`,
build/test, and inspect the JSON plan before deploying. Back up SQLite with the
agent stopped before upgrades; an older agent may not read a migrated database.

